"Salvage - A Personal Odyssey | Book by Ian Tew | Official Publisher Page | Simon & Schuster (https://www.simonandschuster.com/books/Salvage-A-Personal-Odyssey/Ian-Tew/9781574092561)\nciteturn20search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Ian Tew joined Selco Salvage of Singapore in 1974, and spent over a decade on the front line. ...   * Publisher: Sheridan House (November 30, 2007) ...   * Book Cover Image (jpg): Salvage - A Personal Odyssey\n\n  * LOOK\n\nImage: Salvage - A Personal Odyssey\n\nImage: Salvage - A Personal Odyssey\n\n# Salvage - A Personal Odyssey\n\nBy Ian Tew\n\nPublished by Sheridan House\n\nDistributed by Simon & Schuster\n\nTrade Paperback\n\n  * eBook\n\nTrade Paperback\n\nLIST PRICE $40.00\n\nPRICE MAY VARY BY RETAILER\n\nFree shipping when you spend $40. Terms apply.\n\nBuy from Other Retailers\n\n  *   *   *   *   *   *   * ## Table of Contents\n\n  * Rave and Reviews\n\n## About The Book\n\n'No cure, no pay'- those are the terms under which a salvor operates, and in doing so he takes on an onerous responsibility. If he is defeated by the elements he is not paid. He receives nothing, however much money, effort, sweat and tears he has put in. Salvage is not a business for the faint-hearted.\nIan Tew joined Selco Salvage of Singapore in 1974, and spent over a decade on the front line. Already an experienced master mariner, he learnt the salvage trade in the busy waters of the Far East before rising to command some of the world's largest supertugs, eventually becoming a roving salvage master. In his odyssey he roamed the world, from the coast of Cornwall to the Southern Ocean, from the Gulf of Suez to the dangerous reefs of the South China Sea.\nThis is a vivid account of those ten tough years - successes, failures, tows and rescues - a barge adrift in a hurricane in the English Channel - a freighter aground on a reef hundreds of miles from land with a tropical storm approaching - a trawler battered by the surf on a coral reef, its bottom ripped out - a tanker hit by a missile in the Gulf during the 'Tanker War' of the 1980s.\nThe tugs themselves play a big part in the story, as do the crews and captains the author worked with. This gripping account of drama at sea is a tribute to the seamanship, courage and resourcefulness of the salvor, and an insight into the technical, commercial and human issues behind the headlines.\n\n## About The Author\n\nIan Tew\n\n## Product Details\n\n  * Publisher: Sheridan House (November 30, 2007)\n  * Length: 296 pages\n  * ISBN13: 9781574092561\n\n\n## Raves and Reviews\n\nHere is an inside look into the world of salvage; the heroic work and sterling seamanship that makes this such an adventurous career. It is a well-written look into that shaded world of No Cure, No Pay! This adventure on the high seas is all true, and will take you where, hopefully, you won't meet the author\n\n– Latitudes & Attitudes\n\n## Resources and Downloads\n\n### High Resolution Images\n\n  * Book Cover Image (jpg): Salvage - A Personal Odyssey\n\nTrade Paperback 9781574092561\n\n# Salvage - A Personal Odyssey\n\nBy Ian Tew\n\nPublished by Sheridan House\n\nDistributed by Simon & Schuster\n\nTrade Paperback\n\n  * eBook\n\nTrade Paperback\n\nLIST PRICE $40.00\n\nPRICE MAY VARY BY RETAILER\n\nFree shipping when you spend $40. Terms apply.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSalvage: A Personal Odyssey (https://www.bairdmaritime.com/work-boat-world/salvage-a-personal-odyssey)\nciteturn20search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.0 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # Salvage: A Personal Odyssey ... 17 Oct 2008, 4:48 am ... Fortunately, and somewhat unusually, Captain Tew took or obtained a large number of very good photographs.\n\nWork Boat World\n\n# Salvage: A Personal Odyssey\n\nImage: salvage\n\nsalvage\n\nPublished on: \n\n17 Oct 2008, 4:48 am\n\nAuthor: Captain Iain Tew\nPublisher: Seafarer Books\n\nFrom Baird Maritime:\n\nDescribing the decade the author spent working as a salvage master for the once great Selco Salvage from 1974, the book initially focuses on Asia. It expands to describe his adventures as a global roaming salvage master/consultant.\n\nFortunately, and somewhat unusually, Captain Tew took or obtained a large number of very good photographs. They and a first rate painting really enhance this book.\n\nSalvage has become a rather romantic topic over the years, perhaps because of its rescue connotations. It is a risky business that can, sometimes, be very rewarding in both experience and treasure.\n\nFrom a very lengthy seafaring background, the author took to a maritime career naturally. He naturally gravitated toward smaller vessels especially tugs. His sense of adventure obviously drew him to the fascinating world of salvage.\n\nHe has done a great job describing his salvage odyssey. A very personal approach but one that brilliantly describes a great life well lived.\n\nOrdering Information:\n\nSeafarer Books\nRendlesham, UK\nWeb: www.seafarerbooks.com\n\nseafarer\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Case of IBM 386 PC: A Detective Story for Techies by Jim Grep | Goodreads (https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/49388283-the-case-of-ibm-386-pc)\nciteturn20search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.9 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # The Case of IBM 386 PC: A Detective Story for Techies ... Why did a DOS computer game developed in C++ fail only on a particular model of IBM 386 PC? ... Create a free account to discover what your friends think of this book!\n\n# The Case of IBM 386 PC: A Detective Story for Techies\n\n### \n\nJim Grep\n\n3.69 78 ratings6 reviews\n\nWant to Read\n\nBuy on Amazon\n\nRate this book\n\nA Nostalgic Programming Story from Silicon Valley of the 90sA few PC programmers have some fun solving interesting computer bugs entirely by logic and deduction. They take turns narrating an interesting bug story from their past that others try to solve by thinking like an armchair detective.The team gets a thrill hunting down clues in the story narration of three mysterious problems. Why wouldn't the mainframe computer accept a password from a manager? Why did a DOS computer game developed in C++ fail only on a particular model of IBM 386 PC? What was the mysterious bug that waited for several months before striking a happy little C program?The first mystery can be enjoyed by any computer enthusiast. It doesn't need technical knowledge. The second mystery has a problem that is simple to understand but the thinking that leads to the solution turns out to be an interesting exercise. The third story delves lightly into the history of C where the team solves a lightweight but interesting C programming bug.The introduction contains references to some nice armchair detective story collections by Agatha Christie and Isaac Asimov that inspired this story.\n\n46 pages, Kindle Edition\n\nPublished December 16, 2019\n\n\n* * *\n\n### About the author\n\n#### Jim Grep\n\n1 book\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\n## Ratings & Reviews\n\nWhat do you think?\n\nRate this book\n\nWrite a Review\n\n* * *\n\n### Friends & Following\n\nCreate a free account to discover what your friends think of this book!\n\n### Community Reviews\n\n3.69\n\n78 ratings 6 reviews\n\n5 stars\n\n25 (32%)\n\n4 stars\n\n18 (23%)\n\n3 stars\n\n24 (30%)\n\n2 stars\n\n8 (10%)\n\n1 star\n\n3 (3%)\n\nDisplaying 1 - 6 of 6 reviews\n\nTrajano Lima\n\n83 reviews\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nJuly 17, 2021\n\nThe title is misleading. As an avid mystery reader and tech geek, I thought I had found a gem. It was disappoint to figure out the story is about debugging, not mystery. In 26 pages, Jim Grep tells the story of ABC, a group of nerds that challenge each other to solve three programming issues. The stories happen in the 80's, at a pizza restaurant in California. If DOS, CGA and PL/1 doesn't ring any bell to you, forget this book. I hope there will be a sequel where the ABC will get into a real mystery plot using a hacked TRS-80 to decode an encrypted ASCII message from a screeching modem and find a clue hidden in a ROM. EOF().\n\nLike\n\n\nKrystyna\n\n5,134 reviews 55 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nDecember 18, 2019\n\nInteresting set of problems\nI am by no means a computer expert but I found these two problems fascinating and loved the way the team bounced ideas off each other to get to the solution. However I remember my first computer and how you could only play the compatible games on it and that's probably why this tweaked my interest.\n\nLike\n\nComment\n\nLaurence\n\n26 reviews\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nDecember 21, 2019\n\nGreat fun for someone who has worked with these machines.\nI started with mainframes in the late 70s and ran a hybrid shop for many years using cpm, DOS and Windows so the story made sense. BUT, you have to a computer \"geek\" to enjoy them . Not for everyone but great fun for anyone who spent countless hours providing customer support for all flavors of pcs.\n\n\nGary\n\n633 reviews 7 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nMay 3, 2021\n\nActually three stories\nThe format of this story surprised me, but it was actually quite enjoyable. I followed much of the technical jargon, but many details were unfamiliar. The interaction of the characters was fun to watch, too. I was a techie myself years ago, but the problems presented in this story were outside my experience. Nonetheless, I enjoyed the quick read.\n\n\n* * *\n\nMelindeeloo\n\n3,285 reviews 158 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nJuly 19, 2021\n\n2stars - ok, very lightweight. I thought the title was funny when this came up as a freebie - it seemed so nerdy and I am thinking nerdy as when that was an insult. The stories were fitting though since they required knowledge of old hardware to solve in one case. There was some old programming history thrown in. If you want to get an idea of your grandpa/grandma's programming world ....\n\n\nMargaret Urvek\n\n17 reviews 1 follower\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nFebruary 14, 2021\n\nGreat techie for non-techies\nFun stories with great tidbits of computer history. I am not a programmer but these are written in such a way that I had no problems understanding them.\n\nLike\n\nComment--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReviews (https://www.cnrs-scrn.org/northern_mariner/vol19/tnm_19_88-124.pdf)\nciteturn20search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.8 years ago; Crawled: 14.6 years ago; Schokkenbroek has produced a major contribution to maritime history in his *Trying-out, An Anatomy of Dutch Whaling and Sealing in the Nineteenth Century, 1815-1885*. ... Rendlesham, Suffolk: Seafarer Books, 2007. 293 pp., illustrations, maps. ... Captain Ian Tew joined Selco Salvage of Singapore in 1974, and *Salvage* is his account of the next ten or eleven years of his life spent largely in the busy waters of the Indian Ocean, the Persian Gulf and the Far East. ... Tew’s years with Selco were, as he says, a personal odyssey during which he matured, gained experience, and learned the demanding salvage business before rising to command some of the world’s supertugs.\n\nJoost C.A. Schokkenbroek has produced a major contribution to maritime history in his *Trying-out, An Anatomy of Dutch Whaling and Sealing in the Nineteenth Century, 1815-1885*. There is no question that this book should be included in the orders for every significant library collection since it represents a monumental addition to the maritime literature of the subject and the period.\n\nWilliam Henry Flayhart III,  \nDover, Delaware\n\nCaptain Ian Tew. *Salvage, A Personal Odyssey*. Rendlesham, Suffolk: Seafarer Books, 2007. 293 pp., illustrations, maps. UK £ 19.95, US $ 24.95, paper; ISBN US 978-1-57409-256-1.\n\n“No Cure, No Pay,” those are the terms of the Lloyds Open Form of salvage agreement under which a salvor operates. When he takes on the onerous responsibility to salvage a ship, he receives nothing however much money, effort and sweat he puts into the job, if the elements or any other cause defeat him. Salvage is no business for the faint hearted. Captain Ian Tew joined Selco Salvage of Singapore in 1974, and *Salvage* is his account of the next ten or eleven years of his life spent largely in the busy waters of the Indian Ocean, the Persian Gulf and the Far East.\n\nThe author descends from a seafaring family. His grandfather, Cdr. R. D. Graham, was a renowned transatlantic sailor and his mother sailed across the Atlantic at the age of 88. Ian went to sea as a youth, joining the British India Steam Navigation Company. He was a junior cadet officer onboard the *Dara* when an extremist’s bomb blew up the vessel in the Persian Gulf, leading to largest loss of life on a British ship in peacetime since the *Empress of Ireland* (not the *Titanic* as stated here): 238 people perished. It was a harsh introduction to the adult world. After obtaining his second mate’s certificate, Tew served with the Ellerman Line and the Indo-China Steam Navigation Company before gaining his foreign-going master’s certificate at age 25. He joined Selco six years later. Tew’s years with Selco were, as he says, a personal odyssey during which he matured, gained experience, and learned the demanding salvage business before rising to command some of the world’s supertugs.\n\nThe book’s contents are arranged in--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Pilot Boat: Maritime Book Review 2 - \"Salvage - A Personal Odyssey\" (https://thepilotboat.blogspot.com/2008/09/maritime-book-review-2-salvage-personal.html)\nciteturn20search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; ## Sunday, September 14, 2008### Maritime Book Review 2 - \"Salvage - A Personal Odyssey\" ... “Salvage, A Personal Odyssey” by Captain Ian Tew, is a fascinating account of several impressive salvage and towage operations conducted by him during the time he worked for Singapore based salvage company Selco.\n\n## Sunday, September 14, 2008\n\n### Maritime Book Review 2 - \"Salvage - A Personal Odyssey\"\n\nFor my second book review I chose one that I have recently finished reading, although due to several ongoing projects, took me considerably more time doing it than it was anticipated. “Salvage, A Personal Odyssey” by Captain Ian Tew, is a fascinating account of several impressive salvage and towage operations conducted by him during the time he worked for Singapore based salvage company Selco.\n\nImage\nThroughout the book different grasping stories take us immediately to the sea, aboard one of the salvage tugs, with Captain Tew in command. His writing style will certainly be recognized by a professional mariner, such is the attention to details such as the weather conditions and the accurate timing of events that others would consider superfluous.\nThe salvage operations take place mostly in the Indian Ocean, China Sea and Persian Gulf and range from the refloating of grounded cargo vessels on coral reefs to major salvage operations on blazing missile hit VLCC’s during the Iran-Iraq war.\n\nImage\n\nImage\nThe book is full of impressive photographs, diagrams, maps and sent/received telexes during the operations. Captain Tew’s reports take us inside a branch of the shipping industry (salvage) that is commonly misunderstood by other mariners. In fact, as any other shipping business, profiting is the aim, although in this case there is an extreme risk associated with it. The author explains us the aspects of the LOF (Lloyd’s Open Form of salvage agreement) particularly the “no cure - no pay” part. The technical details of the salvage operations are exhaustive and the different salvage tugs play a main role on the stories.\n\nImage\nI found the book very interesting and somehow it contributed to a change in the way I saw salvage. I was once involved, as a ship officer, in a major salvage operation of a holed VLCC and could witness from the signing of the LOF by the master to actively participate on the towage and STS operation that followed. This book reminded me that, although the signing of the LOF can be a dramatic event, from the legal and financial point of view of the ship owner, for the me it was quite a relief to see the salvage tug on the horizon on that morning off the South African coast, back in 1996…\nMy book is signed by the author, copy number 146 of a limited edition of 500.\n\nTitle: Salvage; A Personal Odyssey.\nAuthor: Ian Tew\nPages: 293\nEdition: Seafarer Books\nISBN: 13:9781906266004\nAll photos on this post copyright Captain Ian Tew.\n\nAll books appraised here are owned by this blog author, some were offered, most were bought. There is no commercial intention whatsoever, the only purpose of this blog section is to divulge one of my favorite things (books) concerning my favorite theme (the sea).\nIf there is any copyright infringement on any of the posts let us know at once.\nYou can find Maritime Book Review 1, “Lisbon Docks and Ships” by Luís Miguel Correia here.\n\nPosted by Luis Daniel Vale at 13:26 (2008-09-14T13:26:00+02:00)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(2008) 2 \nEEGESCHIEDENIS (https://www.zeegeschiedenis.nl/wp-content/uploads/2019/11/2008_2_klein.pdf)\nciteturn20search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; TEW, IAN, Salvage: a personal odyssey. ... peter (UK), Edwin Mellen, 2005, x, 129 p., ill. ... the history of discovery.\n47523. THIJS, R., De schipbreuk van de kotter Prin¬ \ncess of Wales en de schoener 1’Aventure (IX). \nNeptunus 50, 2007, 163-165, ill. (vervolg van nr. \n46466) \n47524. WELLS, JAMIN, Professionalization and cul¬ \ntural perceptions of marine salvage, 1850-1950. \nNM 17, 2007, no. 2,1-22, ill. (met Franse samenvat¬ \nting) \n47525. ZEEMAN, SIEP, Brand op de ‘Bavaria’. BW \n63, 2008,118-120, ill. \n47526. -, Zeilperiode in het 